  <subsection xml:id="sec-course-info">
    <title>Course Information</title>

    <p>
      This is the syllabus for <term> (MTH 110-33)</term> 
      for Fall 2026. It is a 4-credit course.
      <!-- use a description list (dl) for short items -->
      <dl>
        <li>
          <title>Instructor</title>

          <p>
            Prof. Firas Hindeleh, MAK A-2-116, <phone>(616) 331-3739</phone>, 
            <url href="mailto:hindelef@gvsu.edu">hindelef@gvsu.edu</url>.
                      </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Student Hours\ availability </title>

          <p>
            I will be holding my office hours on TR 10-10:50AM at my office and on Mondays 10-10:50 at the Peer Collaboration Space PCS (MAK A-2-610). Outside office hours, feel free to stop by my office. Please note that I typically only check email and other messages between 8am and 5pm on weekdays. If you send a message that needs a response during those times, you can expect to get a response within 6 hours. 
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Class meets</title>

          <p>
            TR 11:00-12:50PM MAK D2-329 .
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Course Description</title>

          <p>
            A symbolic, numeric, and graphic approach to intermediate algebra with an emphasis on applications. Topics include operations, equations, and inequalities of linear, exponential, logarithmic, quadratic, rational, and radical functions. Please see the mathematics program for placement test details.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Prerequisite</title>

          <p>
            MTH 097 or assignment through GVSU Math Placement. If you think you have been placed in the wrong course, please see me after class today to discuss your placement. You can take a practice proficiency exam: <url href="gvsu.edu/s/Ik">gvsu.edu/s/Ik</url> .To learn about how to take the exam watch: <url href="gvsu.edu/s/jq">gvsu.edu/s/jq</url>. To take the actual proficiency exam: <url href="gvsu.edu/s/jk"> gvsu.edu/s/jk</url>. It is free and I will not know that you took it.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Textbook (Optional for reference) </title>

          <p>
            <pubtitle>Intermediate Algebra, Functions and Authentic Applications</pubtitle> by Lehmann published by Pearson, 5th edition.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>
    <!-- Use paragraphs to break apart subsections -->
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Topics</title>

      <p>
        
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>Reframing Mathematical Mindset (1-2 week) </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>Exponential Functions (3 - 5 weeks) </p>
          </li>

           <li>
            <p>Inverse Functions and Logarithmic Functions (2 - 4 weeks) </p>
          </li>

           <li>
            <p>Quadratic Functions (3 - 5 weeks) </p>
          </li>

           <li>
            <p>Rational and Radical Functions (1 - 2 weeks)</p>
          </li>

        </ul>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
     <paragraphs>
      <title>Course Objectives</title>
      <p>After successful completion of the course, students will be able to… 
        <ol marker="1.">
          <li>
            Analyze realistic or real-world situations in a mathematical way, using exponential and quadratic models. 
          </li>
        

        
          <li>
            Select appropriate variables to represent the functional inputs and outputs for a mathematical model of a situation using exponential and quadratic models. 
          </li>
        

        
          <li>
            Create an appropriate mathematical model from a description or data, using, if necessary, appropriate technological tools for exponential and quadratic models. 
          </li>
        

        
          <li>
            Solve problems involving exponential, logarithmic, and quadratic equations. 
          </li>
        

        
          <li>
           Translate between different representations of functions (algebraic, graphical, and tabular) 
          </li>
        

       
          <li>
            Explain the mathematical reasoning involved in solving a problem both orally and in writing. 
          </li>
        </ol>
        </p>
         </paragraphs>
</subsection>
  <!-- Other subsections for larger dividers -->
  <subsection xml:id="sec-coursetech">
    <title>Course Technologies</title>
<dl>
        <li>
          <title>BlackBoard</title>

          <p>
            All announcements, assignments, and other course details will be communicated through the course home page, which you access by going to the Blackboard server <url href="http://lms.gvsu.edu">http://lms.gvsu.edu</url> You will be expected to access the course home page frequently and respond accordingly to announcements and assignments posted there. In addition, it will be essential to be a regular user of email in this course. Be sure your preferred email address has been appropriately updated in your Banner. 
          </p>
        </li>
<li>
  <title>Laptop/Tablet:</title>
  <p>
    Preferably one with a touchscreen that allows writing on the screen with a stylus. If you do not have a laptop\tablet that you can bring to class, please let me know so I can bring a device you can use. 
  </p>
</li>

<li><title>Web Browser</title>
  <p>
    Chrome is the preferred browser. Make sure that you are using your GV Google profile when using school related items. Browsers such as Firefox and Edge are also fine. Safari has many issues with Bb. 
  </p>
</li>
